# @optics/lens — the bidirectional-lens sub-family-root.
#
# Sub-family of `@optics` (parent at `shards/optics.mirror`), sibling
# to `@optics/source` (the active-facet gain-media species). Where
# `@optics/source` names the *energy injection* side of Recognition
# #58's cavity-enhanced spectrometer, `@optics/lens` names the
# *coherent focusing* side — the optical element that discharges a
# bidirectional equivalence between two representations of the
# substrate state.
#
# Substrate-decl carrier for Foster's bidirectional-lens laws (Foster,
# Greenwald, Moore, Pierce, Schmitt 2007, *Combinators for
# Bidirectional Tree Transformations: A Linguistic Approach to the
# View-Update Problem*, ACM TOPLAS 29(3)) — the substrate-side home
# of the (get, put) pair whose PutGet / GetPut / PutPut bilaterals
# license the substrate's parse↔render equivalence.
#
# === First substrate-decl consumer of Mara iter-6 ===
#
# Mara iter-6 landed `docs/specs/shatter-is-the-io-linearization-
# operator.md` (`583b939`) — the recognition-candidate that
# `@shatter × @io = linearization operator`. Bidirectional-lens is
# the *shape* that recognition takes at substrate-decl altitude;
# this shard is the first prism-family declaration to carry it.
# The `in @io` implicit-parent is load-bearing: the lens is the
# *transformer* whose forward direction (get) crosses the @io fault
# plane at the substrate-to-target-format boundary, and whose
# backward direction (put) closes the autopoietic loop on return.
#
# === Closing Blocker 2 (autopoietic-closure hop) ===
#
# Alex directive 2026-07-11 named the gap:
#
#   "The gap between @fate and linear formats like the diff are
#    @shatter. From where I'm standing we merely need @shatter/
#    lens'es and the first is @shatter/lens/diff"
#
# Reed grep-check refactored: no @shatter family-root; the parent
# family-root at optical-altitude IS @optics; hence @optics/lens.
# The `put` direction is the *autopoietic-closure hop* that closes
# Blocker 2 (peer_beam returns envelope, not diff bytes): a lens's
# `put(edited_diff, base_state)` integrates an operator-edit back
# into a peer's `@bauchladen.tray`, discharging the feedback loop
# that a one-directional linearization cannot close on its own.
#
# === @mirror/lens vs @optics/lens (Mara+Taut converged) ===
#
# `@mirror/lens` is the *transport* family — the terminal/session
# surface prisms (`@mirror/lens/cli`, `@mirror/lens/shell`,
# `@mirror/lens/mcp`, `@mirror/lens/lsp`) that render one algebra
# for one audience per docs/specs/the-convergence.md §1. Namespace-
# parent only.
#
# `@optics/lens` is the *optical* family — the bidirectional-lens
# altitude at which Foster laws license the equivalence between two
# representations of the substrate state. Foundational, law-carrying.
#
# Both Mara (spec §5, `55221c1`) and Taut (scout Q1 + Q3, `5222333`)
# converged on this distinction independently. The names collide at
# the token `lens` but the families are structurally disjoint: one
# names *which channel* the substrate speaks through, the other
# names *what algebraic shape* the two-directional projection carries.
#
# === Relationship to @shatter (compositional, NOT obsolete) ===
#
# Alex named the gap as "@shatter". Reed's grep-check found no
# `@shatter` family-root; the sub-family properly homes at
# `@optics/lens`. But `@shatter` species remain load-bearing at
# their altitude: `shards/mirror/shatter.mirror` names the graph↔
# linear projection for the on-disk `.shatter`-format five-section
# serialization; `shards/smarts/shatter.mirror` names the smarts-
# altitude read/write pair. Under this landing, `@shatter`'s render
# and parse operations live at species altitude as *one instance*
# of the (get, put) pair whose laws @optics/lens witnesses at the
# family altitude. The forward-promise in
# `shards/mirror/lens/cli/shatter.mirror` (declared `in @optics`
# since 2026-06-12, 30-day forward-promise) resolves at THIS tick:
# the parent family exists.
#
# === Ancestry rationale ===
#
# - `in @prism`   the five-op algebra ancestor (every family-root).
# - `in @glass`   verdict / transparency carriers — Foster laws
#                 return `verdict` in this substrate's shape
#                 (bilateral property discipline, per
#                 shards/glass.mirror).
# - `in @meta`    substrate-decl altitude (boot/01-meta.mirror).
# - `in @nl`      `# <text>` -> nl_literal for docblock terms
#                 (per shards/nl.mirror).
# - `in @optics`  PARENT family-root; @optics/lens sits as sub-
#                 family in its schematic vocabulary. Per
#                 shards/optics.mirror §"Species under this family",
#                 additional sub-families land as the cascade
#                 pulls them; this tick pulls @optics/lens.
# - `in @io`      the linearization crossing — Foster laws bridge
#                 semantic (graph/non-linear) ↔ linear (byte-carrier)
#                 representations, and the linear side lives at the
#                 @io fault plane per Mara iter-6 (`583b939`).
#
# === Family-root declaration ===
#
# Per `[[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]]`, the
# `prism @optics/lens { ... }` block declares the family IS a prism.
# The five-op algebra recurses: each op yields a `lens`-shaped
# artifact at this altitude. Sub-species (e.g. @optics/lens/diff,
# @optics/lens/shatter, @optics/lens/mcp) specialize by fixing the
# semantic and linear carriers.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/optics-lens-family-and-diff-species.md
#               (Mara iter-24, `55221c1`; §1 declares this family's
#               shape, §5 relationship to @shatter, §8 gaps).
# Substrate decisions:
#   [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#   [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#   [[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-mathematics]] (recognition
#      #57; the @io boundary the linear side lives at),
#   [[architecture-bateson-form-behaviour-partition]] (@optics
#      declares the form-side schematic; the lens laws license
#      the process-side dynamics @kintsugi discharges).
# Foster math: docs/math/2026-07-07-shatter-as-bidirectional-lens.md
#              §3.1 (symmetric-lens adaptation), §3.2 (OID-equality
#              at content-address altitude), §3.3 (lens laws IS the
#              2-cell coherence), §3.4 (substrate-decl types).
# Companion recognition: Mara iter-6 (`583b939`) — @shatter × @io =
#   linearization operator; THIS shard is its first substrate-decl
#   consumer at family altitude.
# Boundary scout: Taut iter-12 (`5222333`) — LRM GREEN β verdict on
#   @optics hosting compatibility and Foster-lens spec-only
#   availability.
# Related shards: optics.mirror (the parent family-root),
#   mirror/lens.mirror (the sibling namespace-parent for transport
#   lenses, NOT this shard's parent),
#   mirror/shatter.mirror (one species-altitude witness of the
#   (get, put) pair whose laws this family declares),
#   glass.mirror (verdict / transparency shape returned by
#   bilateral properties).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/optics/lens.mirror`
# declares `@optics/lens` as the sub-family-root; species live at
# `shards/optics/lens/<name>.mirror` (e.g. @optics/lens/diff, landed
# separately by sibling Mara iter-26 as the first species).

# === Type carriers (Foster 2007 shape) ===
#
# The semantic ↔ linear pair. `lens_get` names the semantic → linear
# direction (the render arrow of Reed math §3.4). `lens_put` names
# the linear + old_semantic → new_semantic direction (the parse-
# and-integrate arrow, the autopoietic feedback that closes
# Blocker 2). `lens_witness` names the pair (get, put) together
# with the Foster-law bilaterals that promote it into a
# well-behaved lens.
#
# Per Reed math §3.2, equality is byte-equality on `@mirror/store`
# OIDs at the content-address altitude — the substrate CAN check
# these laws without full pipeline replay. The `ref` carrier is
# the content-address handle; species discharge the specific OID
# provenance (per shards/mirror/store.mirror).

type lens_get = ref
type lens_put = ref
type lens_witness = ref

# === Actions (Foster get / put) ===
#
# `get(semantic) -> linear` — the forward direction; projects the
# semantic (graph, non-linear internal shard) into the linear
# byte-carrier at the @io fault plane. This is `focus` in the
# five-op mapping per Mara iter-24 §1: focus in physical optics
# names the converging pass, semantic → linear = converge onto a
# single byte-carrier.
#
# `put(linear, old_semantic) -> new_semantic` — the backward
# direction; integrates an operator-edit on the linear artifact
# back into the semantic representation. This is `settle` in the
# five-op mapping per Mara iter-24 §1: settle names the reflecting-
# back-and-stabilizing pass, linear + base → new_semantic = fixed-
# point closure. THIS is the autopoietic-closure hop that closes
# Blocker 2: `put(edited_diff, base_bauchladen)` integrates the
# operator's edit back into the peer's tray.
#
# The obligation block (`\ {}`) is STUB at this substrate-decl
# tick; species discharge the concrete implementation. Per two-tick
# discipline (§9 of Mara iter-24 spec), this tick lands the
# admissibility; the discharge lands at species-tick (@optics/lens/
# diff via sibling Mara iter-26; @optics/lens/shatter later).

get(semantic: ref) -> ref { \ {} }
put(linear: ref, old_semantic: ref) -> ref { \ {} }

# === Bilateral properties (Foster 2007 §3 lens laws) ===
#
# The three laws that promote a bare (get, put) pair into a proper
# lens. Each returns `verdict` (per shards/glass.mirror's bilateral
# property discipline); the equality on the RHS is byte-equality
# on `@mirror/store` OIDs per Reed math §3.2. Species-altitude
# consumers discharge the witness at their own OID altitude.
#
# `put_get` (PutGet) — the FIRST well-behaved law. `get(put(v, s))
# == v` for all v, s: putting a linear v back into a base s and
# then getting the linear again recovers v. The lens's forward
# arrow is the RIGHT inverse of the backward arrow. Foster §3
# names this as the *acceptance* law: the lens accepts any linear
# view without silent modification.
#
# `get_put` (GetPut) — the SECOND well-behaved law. `put(get(s), s)
# == s` for all s: getting the linear view of s and then putting
# it back into s (unchanged) leaves s unchanged. The round-trip
# on an unedited view is identity. Foster §3 names this as the
# *stability* law: the lens does not spuriously edit the semantic
# when nothing changed on the linear side.
#
# `put_put` (PutPut) — the "very well-behaved" law. `put(v', put(v,
# s)) == put(v', s)` for all v, v', s: successive puts collapse to
# the last put. The lens's backward arrow is *insensitive to
# intermediate history*. Foster §3 marks this as the strongest
# behavioural law; the two well-behaved laws license most useful
# lenses, and put_put licenses the tree-invariant subset.
#
# Perturbation argument `p` per shards/epistemologic/cybernetic/
# (bilateral property discipline): the law-check is witnessed
# under a specified perturbation regime — for Foster lenses, the
# perturbation is edits to the linear view (the operator's diff).

put_get(l: lens_witness, s: ref, v: ref) -> verdict { \ {} }
get_put(l: lens_witness, s: ref) -> verdict { \ {} }
put_put(l: lens_witness, s: ref, v: ref, v_prime: ref) -> verdict { \ {} }

# === Exports ===

out @optics/lens
out lens_get
out lens_put
out lens_witness
out get
out put
out put_get
out get_put
out put_put

